::CW_U04_L08_GP_Q01::Poem: The bridge vanished in fog, but my feet kept answering the road. Which paraphrase is best?{
~The bridge collapsed forever.#Incorrect. Vanished in fog means hidden, not necessarily collapsed.
~The speaker stopped moving.#Incorrect. The feet kept moving.
=The bridge was hidden by fog, but the speaker kept walking.#Correct. This accurately restates the literal action.
~The road spoke in complete sentences.#Incorrect. Answering is figurative.
}
::CW_U04_L08_GP_Q02::Poem: The calendar still showed April in December. Which interpretation is supported?{
=The speaker may feel emotionally stuck in a past time.#Correct. The outdated calendar supports that idea.
~The calendar proves December comes before April.#Incorrect. That is false and not interpretive.
~The speaker hates all calendars.#Incorrect. Hatred is unsupported.
~The poem is only about math.#Incorrect. It is about time and memory, not math.
}
::CW_U04_L08_GP_Q03::Line: The room held a fragrance of oranges. Which word carries positive connotation?{
~stench.#Incorrect. Stench is negative.
=fragrance.#Correct. Fragrance suggests a pleasant smell.
~odor only.#Incorrect. Odor is more neutral or context-dependent.
~rot.#Incorrect. Rot is negative.
}
::CW_U04_L08_GP_Q04::Poem: The fog erased the street. Which device creates uncertainty?{
~A direct quotation from a source.#Incorrect. No source quotation is involved.
~A cheerful rhyme.#Incorrect. There is no rhyme-based effect here.
~A literal calendar.#Incorrect. No calendar appears.
=Personification of fog as erasing.#Correct. Erased gives fog an action that hides the street.
}
::CW_U04_L08_GP_Q05::Revision task: Change moved to drifted in The light moved across the floor. What improves?{
~The line becomes harsher.#Incorrect. Drifted is not harsh.
~The poem loses all mood.#Incorrect. The word adds mood.
=The diction becomes softer and more precise.#Correct. Drifted suggests gentle movement.
~The sentence becomes a question.#Incorrect. The punctuation does not change.
}